  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1155 PM CDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Light Rain Today, Trending Warmer Sunday\n\nScattered rain showers, as a result of warm air and theta-e \nadvection, have made their way through the CWA and are now mostly in \nwestern Wisconsin, north of I-90. Coverage in the scattered rain \nshowers increases this morning as a shortwave trough moves northward \nfrom Texas along with another push of low-level moisture. A couple \nhundred joules of MUCAPE are also shown in local soundings which may \nbring a few rumbles of thunder to the area. While most locations \nshould see light rain today, not much is expected for areas north of \nI-90 where totals are generally less than 0.15\". Areas south of I-90 \nhave the highest chance at seeing more rain, between 20 and 40% \nchance for at least 0.25\" falling by Sunday morning.   \n\nThese showers begin decaying more frequently by the middle of the \nafternoon as the better forcing shifts off to the northeast and by \nthe early to mid evening, most locations should be on the dry side. \nAs for the trick-or-treating forecast, it is a little tricky to say \nthe least. Diminishing showers, especially over western Wisconsin \ncould cause some locations to experience a wetter late \nafternoon/early evening (20 to 40%) then others. Temperatures look \nto remain in the low to mid 50s for this evening. Heading into \nSunday, the trough moves off to the east and ridging builds in. With \nsome impulses leftover and the moisture remaining in place, some \nlight showers may linger Sunday morning, however those chances are \ncurrently around 10 to 15% and the rest of Sunday will be dry and \nwarm. Highs get back into the low to mid 60s which are 10 to 15 \ndegrees above normal.\n\nMonday - Wednesday:  Widespread Rain with Above Normal Temperatures \nContinuing\n\nLongwave troughing over the western US moves over the central US \nearly next week and lingers through much of the week. The focus for \nour rain chances turns to a low pressure system forming in the \ncentral Plains on Monday. This system moves off to the northeast and \nmay potentially linger into the midweek. As it does, an influx of \nmoisture arrives and coinciding with this amount of forcing, a \nprolonged period of rain is expected from Monday into Wednesday. \nNAEFS continues to show the maximum percentile for PWATs on Monday \nevening and the 99.5 percentile for Tuesday morning as PWATs get \ninto the 1.4 to 1.7\" range. Dewpoints also increase into the upper \n50s to low 60s for Monday and Tuesday. Now based on the EFI signal \nand comparing the heaviest rain axis in the ensemble guidance and AI \nguidance, the heavy rain signal largely remains off to our west and \nnorthwest. That being said the latest LREF gives a 20 to 45% chance \nof at least 1\" of rain to fall between Monday morning and Tuesday \nmorning. This does not include the rain that could fall the rest of \nthe day Tuesday and into Wednesday. Some ensemble members do suggest \nthe potential for 2 to 2.5\" to fall, but that is only around a 10% \nof occurring. There is some uncertainty on how long the rain sticks \naround for as some models have the low being more transient with \nother models supporting a slower exit, with the latter meaning the \npotential rain to continue into Wednesday. Overall, it will be a wet \nstart to the week with widespread rainfall likely. There remains a \ngood chance for an inch of rain to occur with low potential for up \nto 2\". \n\nTemperatures for early next week remain above normal with highs in \nthe 60s for Monday and Tuesday and back down to the 50s for \nWednesday. With the continued period of rain, the low temperature \nfor Monday is actually 7 to 10 degrees warmer than our normal high \nfor this time of year as we will be under warm air advection. Cooler \ntemperatures are expected for the end of the week.",
